from pathlib import Path
from docx import Document
from docx.enum.text import WD_COLOR_INDEX
from docx.oxml import OxmlElement
from docx.text.paragraph import Paragraph
P=Path(__file__).parent;PRE=P.parent/'reviewer1_comment5'
def find(d,text):
 a=[p for p in d.paragraphs if text in p.text];assert len(a)==1,(text,len(a));return a[0]
def replace_run(d,old,new):
 a=[r for p in d.paragraphs for r in p.runs if old in r.text];assert len(a)==1,(old,len(a));a[0].text=a[0].text.replace(old,new);a[0].font.highlight_color=WD_COLOR_INDEX.YELLOW
def rewrite(p,text):
 p.clear();p.add_run(text).font.highlight_color=WD_COLOR_INDEX.YELLOW
def after(a,text,style=None):
 e=OxmlElement('w:p');a._p.addnext(e);p=Paragraph(e,a._parent);p.style=style or a.style;p.add_run(text).font.highlight_color=WD_COLOR_INDEX.YELLOW;return p
def before(a,text,style='Normal'):
 e=OxmlElement('w:p');a._p.addprevious(e);p=Paragraph(e,a._parent);p.style=style;p.add_run(text).font.highlight_color=WD_COLOR_INDEX.YELLOW;return p
m=Document(PRE/'Manuscript_comment5_revised.docx')
replace_run(m,'Across the evaluated endpoints and estimators, the clearest and most consistent acquisition improvements were observed for enantiomeric excess, while yield showed more variable trends.','Yield results varied with system and comparator; a common-test analysis gave an average improvement for Fe(P7)Cl but not for the Fe(CMC-Por)Cl aryl cohort.')
a=find(m,'To assess the aryl-only restriction, we also evaluated')
after(a,'For a paired-endpoint analysis, the common-test splits and starts from the ee sensitivity analysis were reused for isolated yield in the same 38 Fe(P7)Cl and 26 Fe(CMC-Por)Cl aryl records. Each endpoint used only its own training labels; Ridge errors were averaged over the same five label budgets and starting pairs. The acquisition weights remained fixed (SI Section S13).')
replace_run(m,'The acquisition score therefore balances three distinct questions: where the model is uncertain, where the chemistry is insufficiently sampled, and which regions of the defined scope remain poorly represented.','The acquisition score combines model disagreement, molecular novelty and family coverage, with novelty also included in the uncertainty term.')
replace_run(m,'indicating that uncertainty and structural coverage contributed additional information under the evaluated objective.','but this comparison does not isolate the contributions of the individual score components.')
p=find(m,'Yield showed a less consistent acquisition response.')
rewrite(p,'The earlier isolated-yield trajectories gave estimator-dependent results and evaluated different remaining substrates under different routes. A common-test replay gives a more direct comparison. For Fe(P7)Cl, source order, uncertainty–diversity and random gave mean held-out yield MAEs of 21.11, 18.28 and 20.85 percentage points; uncertainty–diversity was lower in 17/20 splits against each comparator. For the Fe(CMC-Por)Cl aryl cohort, the corresponding values were 24.38, 24.99 and 24.76, with uncertainty–diversity lower in 8/20 and 6/20 splits, respectively. The overlapping splits are descriptive and do not establish a general advantage or failure (SI Section S13).')
p=find(m,'This endpoint dependence is chemically plausible')
rewrite(p,'The implemented uncertainty score averages model disagreement with molecular novelty; the overall acquisition score therefore places weight on structural novelty through both the diversity and uncertainty terms. It is not a calibrated estimate of yield prediction error, and selecting a novel or uncertain substrate need not improve held-out yield prediction. This provides a possible algorithmic contributor, not a demonstrated cause of the observed differences. Isolated yield can also reflect conversion, competing pathways and isolation, but the available records do not identify their contributions or a chemical mechanism for the results. Endpoint-specific or multi-objective policies remain future work.')
replace_run(m,'The present results most consistently support uncertainty–diversity ordering for learning enantiomeric excess, whereas yield shows stronger estimator dependence.','The acquisition results vary with system, endpoint, estimator and comparator; the present analyses do not establish a consistent advantage for isolated-yield prediction.')
replace_run(m,'The strongest and most consistent acquisition support was observed for ee; the advantage was neither estimator- nor endpoint-universal.','The acquisition advantage was neither estimator- nor endpoint-universal, and common-test yield results differed between the two systems.')
m.save(P/'Manuscript_comment6_revised.docx')
s=Document(PRE/'Supporting_Information_comment5_revised.docx');a=find(s,'Declaration of AI-assisted manuscript preparation')
before(a,'S13 Paired endpoint yield diagnostics','Heading 1')
before(a,'To test the yield concern on common held-out substrates, we reused the 20 overlapping outer splits and three starting pairs per split in Section S8 for both ee and isolated yield. All 38 Fe(P7)Cl and 26 active Fe(CMC-Por)Cl aryl records had numeric ee and yield fields; seven Fe(CMC-Por)Cl audit records were excluded. The published scope captions describe isolated yields. Each endpoint was fitted and selected using only its own labels. Source-order continuation, uncertainty–diversity and three seeded random continuations per start were compared on the same held-out sets (six or five substrates), candidate pools and label budgets 2, 6, 10, 15 and 20. Ridge MAE was averaged across budgets and starts per split; random continuation was averaged across its three runs. The ee predictions and paths were reused from Section S8, while yield was newly replayed using the frozen score and feature set.')
before(a,'Mean held-out yield MAEs for source / uncertainty–diversity / random were 21.11 / 18.28 / 20.85 percentage points for Fe(P7)Cl and 24.38 / 24.99 / 24.76 for Fe(CMC-Por)Cl aryl substrates. Uncertainty–diversity had lower yield error in 17/20 and 8/20 splits against source, and 17/20 and 6/20 against random, respectively. The corresponding ee MAEs were 16.72 / 13.88 / 14.25 and 15.70 / 14.27 / 15.33. These numbers are means over overlapping splits, not the earlier remaining-pool AUC-MAE. Starting-budget errors are identical across routes because the initial pairs are shared; the common-test differences are descriptive, not independent-dataset significance tests.')
before(a,'The frozen score is 0.40 novelty + 0.40 uncertainty + 0.20 family coverage. Uncertainty is itself the average of normalised novelty and normalised disagreement among Ridge, Elastic Net and Tanimoto-kNN predictions. Except for normalisation edge cases, this yields an effective 0.60 novelty + 0.20 disagreement + 0.20 coverage weighting. The uncertainty score is not a calibrated probability or expected yield prediction error. At six labels, the yield- and ee-trained uncertainty–diversity paths selected on average 2.52 of the same four newly acquired substrates in Fe(P7)Cl and 2.98 in Fe(CMC-Por)Cl; this partial overlap does not establish the cause of the yield result.')
before(a,'The curated yield ranges were 21–99% (median 93.5%) and 30–99% (median 75.5%) for Fe(P7)Cl and Fe(CMC-Por)Cl aryl, respectively; ee–yield Spearman correlations were 0.40 and 0.47. These retrospective descriptive summaries do not identify a chemical mechanism or isolate why a policy performed differently. Isolated yield may depend on conversion, competing pathways and isolation, but their contributions were not measured here. The small reported-scope datasets, overlapping splits, Ridge-only comparison and fixed budgets restrict generality. New yield predictions, selections, score components, input hashes and independent checks are retained in the accompanying analysis records.')
a.paragraph_format.keep_with_next=True
a.paragraph_format.page_break_before=True
s.save(P/'Supporting_Information_comment6_revised.docx')
